## App Design Phase (New Projects)

**IMPORTANT**: For new projects or major features, complete the App Design Phase BEFORE writing implementation code.

### Why Design First?

Jumping straight to code leads to:
- Incomplete features discovered late
- Inconsistent UI and missing pages
- Unclear data requirements
- Rework and wasted effort

### When to Run App Design

Run `/app-design` when:
- Starting a new project after tech stack is ready
- Adding a major new feature (multiple pages)
- Implementation keeps going off-track
- You realize you don't have a clear picture of all pages

### What Gets Designed

| Artifact | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Feature Specs | User stories and acceptance criteria |
| Page Inventory | Every page/screen with URL and purpose |
| User Flows | Diagrams of key workflows |
| Component Trees | UI hierarchy for each page |
| Data Requirements | What each component needs |
| Priority Order | What to build first |

### How to Use

```
/app-design
```

This invokes `dev-designer` to guide you through:
1. Feature discovery questions
2. Page inventory creation
3. User flow mapping
4. Component hierarchy design
5. Data requirements definition

**Output:** `.claude/design/app-design.md` - the blueprint for implementation.

### Implementation Flow

```
PROJECT CREATION → APP DESIGN → UI DESIGN → IMPLEMENTATION
    (tech stack)     (features)   (visual)     (code)
                         ↓           ↓
                dev-designer   dev-ui-designer
                         ↓           ↓
                app-design.md   ui-design.md
                                     ↓
                          dev-frontend/dev-backend
                          follow both designs
```

